<h2>Graph and Answer — authentic data from the preceding survey</h2>
<p>Use the categories and counts from the REAL school survey in Lesson 6. Work with your group to choose a consistent interval scale; title and label both axes; check the most and least frequent category. Ask a partner one- and two-step how-many-more/less questions from the real graph. Teacher approval is required.</p>
<p>Teacher-linked <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-8/lesson-7/lesson.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2021 source lesson</a>. The original warm-up image and publisher Cool-down have not been reproduced or independently verified.</p>
<h3>Independently authored FICTIONAL illustration — each interval = 2 students</h3>
<figure aria-label="Fictional scaled bar graph with 12 Space, 8 Animals and 4 Weather responses; each interval means 2 responses.">
<div role="img" aria-label="Space: 12 students in six steps; Animals: 8 students in four steps; Weather: 4 students in two steps.">
<p><strong>Horizontal axis: number of students</strong>, labeled 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12. <strong>Vertical axis: survey topic categories</strong> (Space, Animals, Weather). Each rectangular block represents 2 students.</p>
<div><strong>Space (12):</strong> <span aria-hidden="true">▮ ▮ ▮ ▮ ▮ ▮</span></div>
<div><strong>Animals (8):</strong> <span aria-hidden="true">▮ ▮ ▮ ▮</span></div>
<div><strong>Weather (4):</strong> <span aria-hidden="true">▮ ▮</span></div>
</div>
<figcaption>These 12, 8, 4 values are NOT the actual school survey. Each block means 2 fictional responses.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>One-step: 12 − 8 = <strong>4</strong> more students chose Space than Animals. Two-step: (12 + 8) − 4 = <strong>16</strong> more chose Space OR Animals than Weather. Teacher reviews why these questions and explanations fit an authentic bar graph.</p>
